\section{Scheduling Regions Instead of Individual Blocks}

A basic block's boundaries limit opportunities to overlap independent
instructions. Bernstein and Rodeh's \emph{Global Instruction
Scheduling for Superscalar Machines} uses control and data
dependence information to guide motion between blocks. Muchnick's
\emph{Advanced Compiler Design and Implementation} describes Fisher's
trace scheduling: choose a likely path, schedule along it, and
repair the paths that enter or leave the trace. Likelihood determines
where motion may be profitable; it says nothing about which paths
may be ignored. A region scheduler needs a record of the original
execution condition of each operation, which values are available
at the new location, and which observations an earlier or later
execution can change.

For example, let entry $E$ branch to a hot block $H$ or cold block
$C$, and let a second predecessor $S$ also enter $H$. An integer
calculation in $H$ can execute earlier on the trace $E,H$ if it
has no observable effects, cannot trap, and its inputs are available
in $E$. But hoisting it to $E$ does not define
the value on the side entrance $S\to H$. One solution is to duplicate
$H$ for that entrance (tail duplication), retain the original
calculation in the copy, and have both versions deliver their results
to later users. Another is to compute the result on the side edge
and join the definitions with a phi. Merely seeing that $E$ is the
first block \emph{on the trace} is not proof that it dominates $H$.

\section{Compensation for a Side Exit}

Consider motion in the other direction. Before branching, $E$
computes a pure, nontrapping $t\gets x+y$. Both successors use $t$,
and the branch itself does not. Sinking the computation into $H$
may free a slot before the branch for work on the branch condition.
Then place compensation on $E\to C$, before any cold-path use of
$t$. If $C$ has another predecessor that already supplies its own
value of $t$, compensation at the start of $C$ is wrong: it would
also overwrite the other predecessor's contribution. Split the edge
and rebuild the corresponding phi operands. On the hot and cold
paths out of $E$, precisely one copy now computes the same value
from the same operands as the old operation.

With a second predecessor for $C$, the compensation in
\Cref{fig:visual-237} belongs on the split edge, not in $C$ itself.

