If the hot path saves two cycles and cold compensation costs four
cycles, with hot probability $p$ the expected saving in this simple
model is $2p-4(1-p)=6p-4$. It is positive only for $p>2/3$,
before code-size effects and downstream changes in register pressure.
This estimate assumes the extra cycles and the hot saving already
account for the changed instructions along each path. It illustrates
why trace extension needs a duplication budget as well as a
probability threshold.

\section{Speculation and Its Semantic Limits}

Hoisting an operation above a branch can execute it where it was
absent before. Producing a fresh value by wrapping integer addition
may be harmless if both inputs are defined and the added value goes
unused on the bypass path. Hoisting a load can instead expose a
fault, race, or device access. For example, given a branch to $H$
only when $p$ is nonnull, loading from $p$ unconditionally in $E$
is invalid if the false path originally returned without touching
memory. In addition, nonnullness on the true path alone does not
prove dereferenceability. Compare the effect distinctions of
Chapter~\ref{ch:effect-ir} and the dossier's \emph{Speculative IR}.

A target might offer an instruction form whose false predicate
suppresses the access and its exception, or a speculative load with
a deferred check and recovery. These mechanisms must be modeled
by their actual semantics: suppressing destination writeback alone
does not make a fault or device read disappear. For a deferred
fault, the check on the original path must occur before committing
any use that would expose the speculative result, with the correct
exception behavior on recovery; the bypass path must not raise the
suppressed fault later. Test the trace with a hot valid address,
a bypass invalid address, and a path entering through $S$ with
distinct operands. Also compare the observable effect order on
the faulting original path, not just the final numeric results.

